"Recuperated Brayton cycle: open gas turbine with air and a recuperator, states 1 to 6

 Compressor 1 -> 2, recuperator cold side 2 -> 3, combustor 3 -> 4, turbine 4 -> 5,
 recuperator hot side 5 -> 6, exhaust. The recuperator preheats the compressed air with the
 turbine exhaust, which raises the efficiency at low pressure ratios.

 This example shows a heat exchanger defined by its effectiveness, a user Function (the
 log-mean temperature difference) and property calls inside a Duplicate loop.

 Also in this project:
   Cycle diagram   the flowsheet with the recuperator
   Parametric      efficiency, specific work and recuperator duty over the pressure ratio
   Plots           T-s diagram, and efficiency and specific work against the pressure ratio
   Goal seek       recuperator effectiveness for a thermal efficiency of 38 %
   Optimisation    pressure ratio of maximum efficiency"

"The unit system: C, bar, kJ, kg/s"
$UnitSystem Eng

"A user function: the log-mean temperature difference of a counter-flow exchanger"
Function lmtd(dT_a, dT_b)
  If abs(dT_a - dT_b) < 1e-6 Then
    lmtd := dT_a
  Else
    lmtd := (dT_a - dT_b)/ln(dT_a/dT_b)
  EndIf
End

"---------------------------------------------------------------- inputs"
T[1] = 15 [C];  P[1] = 1.013 [bar]
r_p = 5                        "compressor pressure ratio (recuperated cycles favour low ratios)"
T[4] = 950 [C]                 "turbine inlet temperature"
eps = 0.85                     "recuperator effectiveness"
dp_rc = 0.02;  dp_rh = 0.03    "recuperator cold- and hot-side pressure losses (fractions)"
dp_cc = 0.03                   "combustor pressure loss (fraction)"
eta_c = 0.85;  eta_t = 0.88    "isentropic efficiencies"
m_dot = 5 [kg/s]               "air mass flow"

P[2] = r_p*P[1]
P[3] = P[2]*(1 - dp_rc)
P[4] = P[3]*(1 - dp_cc)
P[6] = P[1]                    // exhaust to ambient
P[5] = P[6]/(1 - dp_rh)        // the turbine exhausts at the pressure the recuperator needs

Duplicate i = 1, 6
  m[i] = m_dot
End

"---------------------------------------------------------------- states
 Every state is written the same way, so a Duplicate loop gives h and s of all six states
 from the T and P that the equations below determine."
Duplicate i = 1, 6
  h[i] = enthalpy(Air, T=T[i], P=P[i])
  s[i] = entropy(Air, T=T[i], P=P[i])
End

"Compressor 1 -> 2"
h_s[2] = enthalpy(Air, P=P[2], s=s[1])
eta_c = (h_s[2] - h[1]) / (h[2] - h[1])

"Turbine 4 -> 5"
h_s[5] = enthalpy(Air, P=P[5], s=s[4])
eta_t = (h[4] - h[5]) / (h[4] - h_s[5])

"Recuperator: same flow on both sides, so the cold side gains what the hot side loses.
 The effectiveness compares the duty with the largest possible one. The cold side, being
 cooler, has the smaller heat-capacity rate, so at best it is heated to the hot inlet
 temperature T[5]."
Q_rec = m[5]*(h[5] - h[6])     {kW}
Q_rec = m[2]*(h[3] - h[2])
h_max = enthalpy(Air, T=T[5], P=P[3])
eps = (h[3] - h[2])/(h_max - h[2])
dT_lm = lmtd(T[5] - T[3], T[6] - T[2])     {dK}
UA = Q_rec/dT_lm               {kW/K}

"---------------------------------------------------------------- performance"
W_c = m[1]*(h[2] - h[1])       {kW}
W_t = m[4]*(h[4] - h[5])       {kW}
Q_in = m[3]*(h[4] - h[3])      {kW}
W_net = W_t - W_c              {kW}
eta_th = W_net/Q_in            "thermal efficiency"
w_spec = W_net/m_dot           {kJ/kg}  "specific work (names are case-insensitive: w_net would be W_net)"
